## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced Terraform design across multi-cloud per-tenant isolation, secret management, and policy enforcement. It also probes testing discipline and scalable module composition.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliases per tenant
- Tenant-scoped remote backends with encryption
- Vault-backed credentials retrieval
- Per-tenant RBAC and network segmentation
- Idempotence testing with Terratest
- Policy gates with OPA or Sentinel

## Code Example

```hcl
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({ cloud = string, region = string }))
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "tenant_${each.key}"
  region = var.tenants[each.key].region
  # fetch creds from Vault here
}

provider "azurerm" {
  alias   = "tenant_${each.key}"
  features = {}
}

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key = "state/${each.key}/terraform.tfstate"
    encryption = true
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the design to support adding a new cloud to the tenant mix?
- How would you structure tests to run in parallel for many tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of multi-account Terraform setups with minimal code duplication, demonstrating proficiency with provider aliases, wrapper modules, and CI gates to enforce environment boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases for multi-account AWS access
- Backend/workspace separation for state isolation
- Wrapper modules for environment context management
- CI gates to enforce production change protections

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "terraform-state-bucket"
    key    = "${terraform.workspace}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "sandbox"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

module "environment" {
  source = "./modules/environment-wrapper"
  providers = {
    aws = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? aws.prod : aws.sandbox
  }
  environment = terraform.workspace
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cloud multi-tenant infra from a single module, balancing reuse with isolation. Targets provider aliasing, per-tenant tagging, and policy-driven gates.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliasing and for_each
- Cross-cloud module composition
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Vault) for prod changes
- CI gates around per-tenant plan validation

## Code Example

```hcl
# skeleton: tenants map and module per-tenant
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({
    cloud  = string
    region = string
  }))
}

module "tenant" {
  for_each = var.tenants
  source   = "./modules/tenant"

  tenant_name = each.key
  cloud       = each.value.cloud
  region      = each.value.region

  # pass per-tenant providers (pseudo-syntax for illustration)
  providers = {
    aws    = aws.${each.value.cloud == "aws" ? "alias" : "default"}
    google = google.${each.value.cloud == "gcp" ? "alias" : "default"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test drift with cross-cloud resources and ensure idempotence? 
- What are trade-offs between centralized tenants state vs per-tenant backends?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-repo data sharing and multi-region provider patterns, plus a practical gating approach to prevent drift.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliasing across regions
- data.terraform_remote_state for read-only cross-repo data
- Per-region backends and workflows
- CI policy gates to enforce drift checks and approvals
- Cross-region resource referencing of VPC/subnet IDs

## Code Example
```javascript
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.3"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "east"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
data "terraform_remote_state" "net" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "infra-network-bucket"
    key    = "network/us-east-1/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
# usage
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  provider    = aws.west
  ami         = "ami-0abcdef1234567890"
  subnet_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.net.outputs.public_subnet_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across repos? 
- How would you handle regional failover and state restoration?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience with governance, cross-repo data sharing, and policy-as-code in real fintech environments.

## Key Concepts
- cost-governance patterns
- terraform_remote_state data source
- policy as code (OPA)
- CI gating of plans

## Code Example
```javascript
# Minimal budget fetch (pseudo-terraform)
data "terraform_remote_state" "budget" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "gov-budgets";
    key    = "costs/${var.env}.tfstate";
    region = "us-east-1";
  }
}
locals {
  monthly_budget = data.terraform_remote_state.budget.outputs["monthly_budget"]
}
```
```javascript
# Minimal OPA policy sketch
package terraform.costs
deny[msg] {
  planned_cost := input.planned_cost
  budget := input.monthly_budget
  planned_cost > budget
  msg = sprintf("Budget exceeded: %v > %v", planned_cost, budget)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to handle multi-region budgets?
- How would you add tests to verify gating behavior in CI?
